To install nginx and configure it to serve files from `/srv/www`, you can follow these steps:

1. **Install nginx**: Use the following command to install nginx:

    ```bash
    sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y nginx
    ```

2. **Create the directory structure**: Create the `/srv/www` directory and any subdirectories you need for your sites.

    ```bash
    sudo mkdir -p /srv/www
    ```

3. **Configure nginx to serve from `/srv/www`**: Edit the default nginx configuration file located at `/etc/nginx/sites-available/default`.

    ```bash
    sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/default
    ```

    Replace the contents of the file with the following configuration:

    ```text
    server {
        listen 80 default_server;
        listen [::]:80 default_server;

        root /srv/www;
        index index.html index.htm index.php;

        server_name _;

        location / {
            try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
        }

        location ~ \.php$ {
            include snippets/fastcgi-php.conf;
            fastcgi_pass unix:/var/run/php/php8.1-fpm.sock;
        }

        location ~ /\.ht {
            deny all;
        }
    }
    ```

    Save and exit the editor (in nano, press `CTRL + X`, then `Y`, then `Enter`).

4. **Restart nginx**: Apply the changes by restarting the nginx service.

    ```bash
    sudo systemctl restart nginx
    ```

5. **Create test files**: Create a simple `index.html` file in `/srv/www` to test if nginx is serving files from the new directory.

    ```bash
    echo "Hello, World!" | sudo tee /srv/www/index.html
    ```

6. **Verify**: Open a web browser and navigate to the IP address or domain name of your server. You should see "Hello, World!" displayed.

This setup configures nginx to serve files from `/srv/www` and includes support for PHP files using PHP-FPM. Make sure you have the necessary PHP package installed:

```bash
sudo apt-get install -y php8.1-fpm php8.1-mysql
```

Replace `php8.1` with the appropriate PHP version if needed.
